};{q}^{2})}_{\\infty }.$\\sum _{i,j,k,l\\ge 0}\\frac{{a}^{i+l}{b}^{j+l}{q}^{\\left(\\begin{array}{c}i+j+k+2l+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\left(\\begin{array}{c}i+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\left(\\begin{array}{c}j+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+l}}{{(q;q)}_{i}{(q;q)}_{j}{(q;q)}_{k}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{l}}={(-q;q)}_{\\infty }{(-a{q}^{2},-b{q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{\\infty }.\nAs for identities involving triple sums or quadruple sums, besides the Kanade-Russell identities of indexes $(1,2,3)$ and $(1,4,6)$ such as (1.9)–(1.12), there are other known identities of indexes $(1,1,6)$, $(1,2,2)$, $(1,2,3)$, $(1,1,1,2)$, $(1,2,2,4)$ and $(1,2,3,4)$. For example, Rosengren [25, Eq. (5.3a)] proved an identity of index $(1,1,6)$. Kanade and Russell [17] presented four conjectural identities of index $(1,2,3,4)$. Takigiku and Tsuchioka [29] proved some identities of indexes $(1,2,2)$ and $(1,2,2,4)$, which are related to the principal characters of the level 5 and level 7 standard modules of the affine Lie algebra {A}_{2}^{(2)}${A}_{2}^{(2)}$. For example, they proved that [29, Theorem 1.3]$\\sum _{i,j,k\\ge 0}\\frac{{q}^{\\left(\\begin{array}{c}i\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+8\\left(\\begin{array}{c}j\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+10\\left(\\begin{array}{c}k\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+2ij+2ik+8jk+i+4j+5k}}{{(q;q)}_{i}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{j}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{k}}=\\frac{1}{{(q,{q}^{3},{q}^{4},{q}^{5},{q}^{7},{q}^{9},{q}^{11},{q}^{13},{q}^{15},{q}^{16},{q}^{17},{q}^{19};{q}^{20})}_{\\infty }}.$\n\nRecently, Mc Laughlin [20] applied Rosengren's method in [25] to derive some new Rogers-Ramanujan type identities including the following one of index $(1,2,3)$ \\sum _{i,j,k\\ge 0}\\frac{{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{(3k+2j-i)(3k+2j-i-1)/2+j(j-1)-i+6j+6k}}{{(q;q)}_{i}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{j}{({q}^{3};{q}^{3})}_{k}}=\\frac{{(-1;q)}_{\\infty }{({q}^{18};{q}^{18})}_{\\infty }}{{({q}^{3};{q}^{3})}_{\\infty }{({q}^{9};{q}^{18})}_{\\infty }}.$\\sum _{i,j,k\\ge 0}\\frac{{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{(3k+2j-i)(3k+2j-i-1)/2+j(j-1)-i+6j+6k}}{{(q;q)}_{i}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{j}{({q}^{3};{q}^{3})}_{k}}=\\frac{{(-1;q)}_{\\infty }{({q}^{18};{q}^{18})}_{\\infty }}{{({q}^{3};{q}^{3})}_{\\infty }{({q}^{9};{q}^{18})}_{\\infty }}.$ Note that in [20], such identities are called as identities of Kanade-Russell type. In the way of finding generalizations of Capparelli's first partition identity, Dousse and Lovejoy [13, Eqs. (2.6),(2.7)] proved the following identity of index $(1,1,1,2)$:\\sum _{i,j,k,l\\ge 0}\\frac{{a}^{i+l}{b}^{j+l}{q}^{\\left(\\begin{array}{c}i+j+k+2l+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\left(\\begin{array}{c}i+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\left(\\begin{array}{c}j+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+l}}{{(q;q)}_{i}{(q;q)}_{j}{(q;q)}_{k}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{l}}={(-q;q)}_{\\infty }{(-a{q}^{2},-b{q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{\\infty }.$\\sum _{i,j,k,l\\ge 0}\\frac{{a}^{i+l}{b}^{j+l}{q}^{\\left(\\begin{array}{c}i+j+k+2l+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\left(\\begin{array}{c}i+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\left(\\begin{array}{c}j+1\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+l}}{{(q;q)}_{i}{(q;q)}_{j}{(q;q)}_{k}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{l}}={(-q;q)}_{\\infty }{(-a{q}^{2},-b{q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{\\infty }.$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProofs of eighteen conjectural identities related to the modulo nine Kanade--Russell identities | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/fr/abs/2609.24129)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; # Proofs of eighteen conjectural identities related to the modulo nine Kanade--Russell identities ... The asymmetric and cyclotomic cases are treated through $q$q-Airy--theta connection formulas, whereas the Hickerson identities are derived from low-order divided differences.\nSubmitted 21 Sept 2026\n\n# Proofs of eighteen conjectural identities related to the modulo nine Kanade--Russell identities\n\nEX Ernest X.W. Xia\n\n## Abstract\n\nUncu and Zudilin studied reflections of finite forms of the modulo nine Kanade--Russell identities under $q\\mapsto q^{-1}$q↦q−1, leading to ten conjectural sum--product evaluations, including two proposed by Warnaar. Hickerson conjectured four further identities in his study of contiguous relations for the associated parameterized double sums. By constructing finite forms of Hickerson's sums and studying their reflections, Konenkov proposed two additional product identities, whose conjugates give two more. In this paper, we prove all eighteen conjectural identities by combining Nahm-sum dual evaluations established by the author with parameterized divided-difference identities. For the symmetric reflections, Schur-type decompositions and finite rational identities reduce the required evaluations to nonsingular two-by-two linear systems. The asymmetric and cyclotomic cases are treated through $q$q-Airy--theta connection formulas, whereas the Hickerson identities are derived from low-order divided differences.\n\nView PaperPDF--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Azhad_Q in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AAzhad_Q+in%3Amath)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; @misc{2508.17854, author = {Gaurav Kottari and Niteesh Sahni and Qazi J.\n  * Structural Characterisations of (n-1,n)-Trees\n\nGaurav Kottari, Niteesh Sahni, Qazi J. Azhad\n\nAug 26 2025 math.CO arXiv:2508.17854v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17854, author = {Gaurav Kottari and Niteesh Sahni and Qazi J.~Azhad}, title = {{S}tructural {C}haracterisations of (n-1,n)-{T}rees}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17854}, note = {arXiv:2508.17854v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study higher-dimensional analogues of graph-theoretic trees within the class of pure n-simplicial complexes. Focusing on the case m = n-1 in Dewdney's (m, n)-tree framework, we introduce refined notions of path and circuit sequences that overcome the structural limitations of existing definitions. Using these refinements,